**Solution Explanation**

The goal is to unit‑test the overloaded `validate` method of `BaseJsonValidator`:

```java
public <T> T validate(ExecutionContext executionContext,
                      JsonNode node,
                      OutputFormat<T> format,
                      ExecutionContextCustomizer executionCustomizer)
```

The method has three responsibilities that can be verified independently:

1. **Customising the `OutputFormat`** – `format.customize(executionContext, validationContext)` must be called.
2. **Customising the `ExecutionContext`** – if an `ExecutionContextCustomizer` is supplied, its `customize` method must be invoked.
3. **Formatting the validation result** – the set of `ValidationMessage`s returned by the protected `validate(ExecutionContext, JsonNode)` method must be passed to `format.format(...)` and the value returned by `format.format` must be the method’s return value.

Because `BaseJsonValidator` is abstract we create a **real‑method mock** (Mockito `CALLS_REAL_METHODS`) that uses the real constructor but allows us to stub the protected `validate(ExecutionContext, JsonNode)` method. All other collaborators (`ExecutionContext`, `JsonNode`, `OutputFormat`, `ExecutionContextCustomizer`, `ValidationMessage`) are mocked.

Two test cases are provided:

* **`testValidateWithoutCustomizer`** – validates the flow when no `ExecutionContextCustomizer` is supplied.
* **`testValidateWithCustomizer`** – validates the flow when a customizer is supplied and ensures it is invoked.

All required imports are added and the JUnit 5 lifecycle methods (`@BeforeAll`, `@BeforeEach`, `@AfterEach`, `@AfterAll`) are present, although they only contain placeholder comments for possible future extensions.

---

```java
package com.networknt.schema;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.mockito.*;
import org.mockito.junit.jupiter.MockitoExtension;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class BaseJsonValidator_validate_3_Test {

    // Mocks that are reused across test methods
    @Mock ExecutionContext mockExecutionContext;
    @Mock JsonNode mockJsonNode;
    @Mock OutputFormat<String> mockOutputFormat;               // we will format to a String
    @Mock ExecutionContextCustomizer mockCustomizer;
    @Mock ValidationMessage mockValidationMessage;

    // The validator under test – a real‑method mock of the abstract class
    BaseJsonValidator validator;

    @BeforeAll
    static void setupBeforeAll() {
        // No global resources required for these tests
    }

    @BeforeEach
    void setupBeforeEach() {
        // Create a real‑method mock of BaseJsonValidator.
        // All constructor arguments are null – they are not needed for the logic we test.
        validator = mock(BaseJsonValidator.class,
                withSettings()
                        .useConstructor(null, null, null, null, (ValidatorTypeCode) null, null)
                        .defaultAnswer(CALLS_REAL_METHODS));

        // Stub the protected validate(ExecutionContext, JsonNode) method to return a known set.
        Set<ValidationMessage> messages = new HashSet<>();
        messages.add(mockValidationMessage);
        when(validator.validate(any(ExecutionContext.class), any(JsonNode.class)))
                .thenReturn(messages);
    }

    @AfterEach
    void teardownAfterEach() {
        // Reset mocks to avoid cross‑test interference
        reset(mockExecutionContext, mockJsonNode, mockOutputFormat, mockCustomizer, mockValidationMessage, validator);
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void teardownAfterAll() {
        // No global resources to clean up
    }

    @Test
    void testValidateWithoutCustomizer() {
        // Arrange
        // format.customize should be called with the execution context and the validator's (null) ValidationContext
        doNothing().when(mockOutputFormat).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());

        // format.format should return a deterministic value that we can assert on
        when(mockOutputFormat.format(anySet(), eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull()))
                .thenReturn("formatted-result");

        // Act
        String result = validator.validate(mockExecutionContext, mockJsonNode, mockOutputFormat, null);

        // Assert
        // 1. The OutputFormat must have been customised
        verify(mockOutputFormat, times(1)).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());

        // 2. No customizer, therefore it must not be invoked
        verifyNoInteractions(mockCustomizer);

        // 3. The internal validate method must have been called exactly once
        verify(validator, times(1)).validate(eq(mockExecutionContext), eq(mockJsonNode));

        // 4. The format method must have been called with the messages returned by the internal validate
        verify(mockOutputFormat, times(1)).format(eq(Collections.singleton(mockValidationMessage)),
                eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());

        // 5. The method must return the value produced by OutputFormat.format
        assertEquals("formatted-result", result);
    }

    @Test
    void testValidateWithCustomizer() {
        // Arrange
        doNothing().when(mockOutputFormat).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());
        when(mockOutputFormat.format(anySet(), eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull()))
                .thenReturn("customised-result");

        // The customizer should receive the same execution context and the validator's ValidationContext (null)
        doNothing().when(mockCustomizer).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());

        // Act
        String result = validator.validate(mockExecutionContext, mockJsonNode, mockOutputFormat, mockCustomizer);

        // Assert
        verify(mockOutputFormat, times(1)).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());
        verify(mockCustomizer, times(1)).customize(eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());
        verify(validator, times(1)).validate(eq(mockExecutionContext), eq(mockJsonNode));
        verify(mockOutputFormat, times(1)).format(eq(Collections.singleton(mockValidationMessage)),
                eq(mockExecutionContext), isNull());

        assertEquals("customised-result", result);
    }
}
```

